@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 5: control flow and errors)

Loops, branches and error handling, with the timing tools that tell you whether to vectorise.

@section(Branches)

`if` picks a branch by a condition; `switch` picks by a value, and a case may list several values.

@octo
x = 7;
if x > 10
  disp("big")
elseif x > 5
  disp("medium")
else
  disp("small")
end
day = "sat";
switch day
  case {"sat", "sun"}
    disp("weekend")
  otherwise
    disp("weekday")
end
@end

@section(Loops)

`for` walks the columns of an array; `while` repeats until a condition fails; `break` and `continue` leave early.

@octo
for k = 1:3
  printf("k = %d\n", k);
end
n = 0;
while true
  n = n + 1;
  if mod(n, 2) == 0, continue, end
  if n > 7, break, end
  printf("%d ", n);
end
printf("\n");
do
  n--;
until n < 5
n
@end

@section(Errors)

`error` stops the program with a message; `try`/`catch` recovers; `warning` reports without stopping.

@octo
try
  error("demo:bad", "value %d is out of range", 42);
catch err
  disp(err.message)
  disp(err.identifier)
end
warning("this is only a warning");
try
  [1 2] * [3 4];
catch e
  printf("caught: %s\n", e.message);
end
lasterr()
@end

@section(Validation)

Check inputs early so the error points at the real cause.

@octo
v = 5;
assert(v > 0, "v must be positive");
validateattributes(v, {"numeric"}, {"positive"});
isnumeric(v) && isscalar(v)
@end

@section(Timing)

`tic`/`toc` time a stretch of code; here a loop and its vectorised version give the same answer.

@octo
tic; s = 0; for k = 1:1e5, s = s + k; end; t1 = toc;
tic; s2 = sum(1:1e5); t2 = toc;
s == s2
t1 >= 0 && t2 >= 0
@end
